1
00:00:00,146 --> 00:00:03,126
Time for the experiment. The book takes one single

2
00:00:03,126 --> 00:00:05,946
string and runs it through one trained tokenizer after

3
00:00:05,946 --> 00:00:09,126
another, so the only thing changing is the tokenizer

4
00:00:09,126 --> 00:00:09,811
itself.

5
00:00:10,393 --> 00:00:13,343
Here are four of them. BERT uncased, which uses

6
00:00:13,343 --> 00:00:19,083
WordPiece. GPT-2, which uses byte pair encoding. FLAN-T5, which

7
00:00:19,083 --> 00:00:23,923
uses SentencePiece. And GPT-4, also byte pair encoding, with

8
00:00:23,923 --> 00:00:25,331
a much larger vocabulary.

9
00:00:26,825 --> 00:00:30,305
Notice the distinction. WordPiece and byte pair encoding are

10
00:00:30,305 --> 00:00:34,115
algorithms. SentencePiece is a framework that can implement more

11
00:00:34,115 --> 00:00:37,655
than one of them. And FLAN-T5 and GPT-4 are

12
00:00:37,655 --> 00:00:40,945
models that own a particular trained tokenizer. Keep those

13
00:00:40,945 --> 00:00:43,611
separate, and the rest of this comparison makes sense.

14
00:00:43,859 --> 00:00:47,229
The book compares eight trained tokenizers in total. We

15
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:49,639
are showing four, because four is enough to see

16
00:00:49,639 --> 00:00:52,651
the disagreements. And they do disagree.

17
00:00:53,365 --> 00:00:56,880
Look at one word from the test string: capitalization,

18
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:58,424
written in capitals.

19
00:00:58,639 --> 00:01:02,969
BERT uncased lowercases its input first. So the capitals

20
00:01:02,969 --> 00:01:06,129
are gone before anything else happens. It then splits

21
00:01:06,129 --> 00:01:10,678
the word into capital, and hash-hash ization. Two pieces.

22
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:14,239
Those double hashes mark a continuation: this token is

23
00:01:14,239 --> 00:01:15,851
joined to the one before it.

24
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:19,134
But BERT also ships in a cased version, and

25
00:01:19,134 --> 00:01:22,698
it does not lowercase. The very same word becomes

26
00:01:22,698 --> 00:01:26,812
eight pieces. Eight. That is four times the cost,

27
00:01:26,812 --> 00:01:27,964
for one word.

28
00:01:28,591 --> 00:01:33,891
GPT-2 keeps the capitals and uses four pieces. FLAN-T5

29
00:01:33,891 --> 00:01:40,711
uses five. GPT-4 uses two: CAPITAL, and IZATION. Same

30
00:01:40,711 --> 00:01:44,004
input, same word, four different answers.

31
00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:46,517
And there is a cost that never shows up

32
00:01:46,517 --> 00:01:49,787
in a token count. BERT uncased throws the newline

33
00:01:49,787 --> 00:01:52,767
characters away completely. Which means a chat log, where

34
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:55,337
every turn starts on a new line, loses that

35
00:01:55,337 --> 00:01:57,571
structure before the model ever sees it.

36
00:01:58,327 --> 00:02:00,417
Now the part of the test string that breaks

37
00:02:00,417 --> 00:02:04,287
the older tokenizers. A musical note, and the Chinese

38
00:02:04,287 --> 00:02:05,718
character for bird.

39
00:02:05,837 --> 00:02:10,067
BERT replaces both with unknown. FLAN-T5 does the same

40
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:12,857
thing, using its own marker. And here is why

41
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:16,616
that matters: the marker records that something was there.

42
00:02:16,616 --> 00:02:19,437
It does not record what. Look the marker up,

43
00:02:19,437 --> 00:02:21,351
and the character is simply gone.

44
00:02:21,473 --> 00:02:24,213
GPT-2 takes a different route. It falls back to

45
00:02:24,213 --> 00:02:27,293
the raw bytes that make up the character. The

46
00:02:27,293 --> 00:02:31,153
musical note is three bytes, and they become three

47
00:02:31,153 --> 00:02:34,273
tokens. Printed on their own they look like replacement

48
00:02:34,273 --> 00:02:35,763
characters, because a single byte is not a readable

49
00:02:35,763 --> 00:02:36,584
character.

50
00:02:36,864 --> 00:02:42,774
Their identifiers are 8582, 236 and 113. Decode them

51
00:02:42,774 --> 00:02:45,564
together, as a group, and the note comes back

52
00:02:45,564 --> 00:02:49,014
intact. The book prints no identifiers for the Chinese

53
00:02:49,014 --> 00:02:51,058
character, so neither do we.

54
00:02:51,248 --> 00:02:54,608
That is the trade. Byte fallback keeps coverage, so

55
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,908
nothing is ever simply lost. But a single character

56
00:02:57,908 --> 00:03:00,558
can cost several tokens, and every one of them

57
00:03:00,558 --> 00:03:02,564
takes a position in the context.

58
00:03:03,268 --> 00:03:07,590
One more kind of input decides the outcome: whitespace.

59
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:10,668
Here is the book's own code example. Those dots

60
00:03:10,668 --> 00:03:14,152
mark four spaces of indentation. They are not code

61
00:03:14,152 --> 00:03:16,524
— they are showing you what is invisible.

62
00:03:16,634 --> 00:03:20,094
Send those four spaces through a character-level tokenizer and

63
00:03:20,094 --> 00:03:23,034
you get four separate tokens. The model then has

64
00:03:23,034 --> 00:03:25,274
to work out that they belong together, and how

65
00:03:25,274 --> 00:03:26,951
deep the indentation is.

66
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:30,720
GPT-4 does something better here. It holds a single

67
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:34,150
token for four consecutive spaces. In fact it has

68
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:37,030
a dedicated token for every run of whitespace up

69
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:40,410
to eighty three. And the Python keyword elif gets

70
00:03:40,410 --> 00:03:41,704
a token of its own.

71
00:03:41,858 --> 00:03:45,988
StarCoder2 is a code specialist, and pushes further. It

72
00:03:45,988 --> 00:03:49,058
gives every digit its own token, so six hundred

73
00:03:49,058 --> 00:03:53,718
becomes three tokens: six, zero, zero. The reasoning is

74
00:03:53,718 --> 00:03:56,788
that numbers and mathematics are represented more clearly that

75
00:03:56,788 --> 00:03:57,231
way.

76
00:03:57,451 --> 00:04:01,371
One caution. This is not parsing. A tokenizer that

77
00:04:01,371 --> 00:04:05,261
handles indentation and keywords well makes the model's job

78
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:07,921
easier. It does not make the code it generates

79
00:04:07,921 --> 00:04:08,771
correct.
